{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373559673,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a11adbb03624dfdf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY' 2>&1 | head -n 80\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n# BR-Football tournament values\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r=csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows=list(r)\nprint(\"BR-Football cols:\", list(rows[0].keys()))\nprint(\"BR-Football tournaments:\", Counter(x['tournament'] for x in rows).most_common(15))\nprint(\"sample date/time:\", rows[0]['date'], rows[0]['time'])\n\n# fifa Nationalities + Brazilian clubs\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    r=csv.DictReader(f)\n    frows=list(r)\nprint(\"fifa cols count:\", len(frows[0]), \"rows:\", len(frows))\nprint(\"fifa Nationalities top:\", Counter(x['Nationality'] for x in frows).most_common(5))\nbrazil=[x['Club'] for x in frows if x['Nationality']=='Brazil']\nprint(\"Brazilian players:\", len(brazil))\nprint(\"top Brazilian clubs:\", Counter(brazil).most_common(15))\nPY"},"output":"BR-Football cols: ['tournament', 'home', 'home_goal', 'away_goal', 'away', 'home_corner', 'away_corner', 'home_attack', 'away_attack', 'home_shots', 'away_shots', 'time', 'date', 'ht_diff', 'at_diff', 'ht_result', 'at_result', 'total_corners']\nBR-Football tournaments: [('Serie A', 3689), ('Serie B', 3677), ('Serie C', 1807), ('Copa do Brasil', 1123)]\nsample date/time: 2023-09-24 20:00:00\nfifa cols count: 89 rows: 18207\nfifa Nationalities top: [('England', 1662), ('Germany', 1198), ('Spain', 1072), ('Argentina', 937), ('France', 914)]\nBrazilian players: 827\ntop Brazilian clubs: [('Grêmio', 20), ('Atlético Mineiro', 20), ('Cruzeiro', 20), ('Fluminense', 20), ('Santos', 20), ('Internacional', 20), ('América FC (Minas Gerais)', 20), ('Botafogo', 20), ('Bahia', 20), ('Paraná', 20), ('Atlético Paranaense', 20), ('Vitória', 20), ('Sport Club do Recife', 20), ('Chapecoense', 20), ('Ceará Sporting Club', 20)]\n","metadata":{"output":"BR-Football cols: ['tournament', 'home', 'home_goal', 'away_goal', 'away', 'home_corner', 'away_corner', 'home_attack', 'away_attack', 'home_shots', 'away_shots', 'time', 'date', 'ht_diff', 'at_diff', 'ht_result', 'at_result', 'total_corners']\nBR-Football tournaments: [('Serie A', 3689), ('Serie B', 3677), ('Serie C', 1807), ('Copa do Brasil', 1123)]\nsample date/time: 2023-09-24 20:00:00\nfifa cols count: 89 rows: 18207\nfifa Nationalities top: [('England', 1662), ('Germany', 1198), ('Spain', 1072), ('Argentina', 937), ('France', 914)]\nBrazilian players: 827\ntop Brazilian clubs: [('Grêmio', 20), ('Atlético Mineiro', 20), ('Cruzeiro', 20), ('Fluminense', 20), ('Santos', 20), ('Internacional', 20), ('América FC (Minas Gerais)', 20), ('Botafogo', 20), ('Bahia', 20), ('Paraná', 20), ('Atlético Paranaense', 20), ('Vitória', 20), ('Sport Club do Recife', 20), ('Chapecoense', 20), ('Ceará Sporting Club', 20)]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY' 2>&1 | head -n 80\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n# BR-Football tournament values\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r=csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows=list(r)\nprint(\"BR-Football cols:\", list(rows[0].keys()))\nprint(\"BR-Football tournaments:\", Counter(x['tournament'] for x in rows).most_common(15))\nprint(\"sample date/time:\", rows[0]['date'], rows[0]['time'])\n\n# fifa Nationalities + Brazilian clubs\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    r=csv.DictReader(f)\n    frows=list(r)\nprint(\"fifa cols count:\", len(frows[0]), \"rows:\", len(frows))\nprint(\"fifa Nationalities top:\", Counter(x['Nationality'] for x in frows).most_common(5))\nbrazil=[x['Club'] for x in frows if x['Nationality']=='Brazil']\nprint(\"Brazilian players:\", len(brazil))\nprint(\"top Brazilian clubs:\", Counter(brazil).most_common(15))\nPY","time":{"start":1788373559659,"end":1788373559667}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I have a good understanding now. The `mcp` v2 `MCPServer` works like FastMCP with a `@tool()` decorator and `run()`. Let me design my implementation.\n\nArchitecture plan:\n1. `brsl/data_loader.py` - loads all 6 CSVs into pandas DataFrames with normalized columns, parses dates, normalizes team names\n2. `brsl/knowledge_graph.py` - in-memory graph (teams, players, matches, competitions) — \"knowledge graph interface\"\n3. `brsl/query_engine.py` - all the query functions (matches, teams, players, competitions, stats)\n4. `brsl/server.py` - MCP server exposing tools using MCPServer\n5. `brsl/__init__.py`\n6. `tests/test_*.py` - BDD GWT structured pytest tests\n7. `pyproject.toml` or `setup.py` for build - let me check what build tooling exists. There's no pyproject yet. Let me create one.\n\nLet me look more closely at the data to understand team name formats and edge cases. Let me look at the BR-Football-Dataset tournament values and the fifa_data Club values for Brazilian clubs, and a few rows of each.\n\nLet me plan the team name normalization:\n- Brasileirao_Matches: \"Palmeiras-SP\" → strip \"-SP\" suffix → \"Palmeiras\"\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches: \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" / \"América - MG\" → these have \" - STATE\" \n- Libertadores: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\" → international teams with country codes in parens\n- BR-Football-Dataset: \"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\" (no accents, no state)\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro: \"Guarani\", \"Vasco\" (no state in name, separate UF columns)\n- fifa_data: Club \"FC Barcelona\", \"Flamengo\" etc.\n\nNormalization strategy:\n- Create a `normalize_team_name(name)` that:\n  - strips whitespace\n  - removes state suffix patterns: trailing \"-XX\" where XX is 2-letter state, or \" - XX\"\n  - removes country code in parens like \"(URU)\" \"(EQU)\" \n  - normalizes accents to a canonical form? No - keep accents but normalize variants. Actually for matching across datasets, the issue is \"Sao Paulo\" vs \"São Paulo\". I'll normalize by removing accents (unicodedata NFD) for a normalized key, while keeping display name as the accented original.\n  - lowercase for matching key\n\nLet me create a normalized key function: `team_key(name)` → lowercase, remove diacritics, remove state/country suffixes, strip.\n\nFor team matching in queries: when searching for \"Flamengo\", normalize and check if any team's key contains/equals the query key.\n\nLet me think about the derbies. The spec mentions \"Show me all derbies in 2023\" — find matches where teams are traditional rivals. I'll define a small set of known Brazilian derby pairs (Fla-Flu = Flamengo/Fluminense, Clássico Majestoso = Corinthians/São Paulo, etc.). This is reasonable for a demo.\n\nNow let me design the query functions to cover the example questions. I'll create a comprehensive set of tools.\n\nLet me start building. First the data loader. I need to understand the data better - let me sample more.\n\nActually I have enough. Let me be efficient and build. Let me look at a few more details: the fifa_data Nationality values for Brazil, and Club values. And the BR-Football-Dataset tournament values.\n\nLet me write the code now. I'll create a package `brsl` (Brazilian Soccer).\n\nLet me think about whether to use pandas or stdlib csv. Pandas is installed and the spec wants performance < 2s for simple, < 5s for aggregates on ~25k rows total. Pandas is fine and cleaner. I'll use pandas.\n\nLet me design the normalized MatchRecord and the loader.\n\nData loading plan per file:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv: cols datetime, home_team, home_team_state, away_team, away_team_state, home_goal, away_goal, season, round → competition=\"Brasileirão Serie A\"\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv: round, datetime, home_team, away_team, home_goal, away_goal, season → competition=\"Copa do Brasil\"\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv: datetime, home_team, away_team, home_goal, away_goal, season, stage → competition=\"Copa Libertadores\"\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv: tournament, home, home_goal, away_goal, away, ... date, time → competition=tournament value\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv: ID, Data, Ano, Rodada, Equipe_mandante, Equipe_visitante, Gols_mandante, Gols_visitante, ..., Vencedor, Arena → competition=\"Brasileirão Serie A\" (historical 2003-2019)\n\nI'll build a unified DataFrame with columns: date (datetime), home_team (normalized display), away_team, home_goal (int), away_goal (int), season (int), competition, round (str), stage (str, optional), home_state, away_state, stadium, source_file.\n\nFor the extended stats file I'll keep additional stat columns.\n\nFor team name normalization:\n- display_name: cleaned accented original (strip state suffix, country code parens)\n- key: ascii-lowercased normalized\n\nLet me write `data_loader.py`.\n\nActually I want to keep the original home_team for display but also have normalized. Let me store both. For the knowledge graph nodes, I'll key by normalized team key.\n\nLet me consider the home_team_state handling: in Brasileirao_Matches the state is already separate. In novo, there are Mandante_UF/Visitante_UF. In Brazilian_Cup, team names have \" - RJ\" suffix; I can extract. In BR-Football and Libertadores, no state.\n\nFor simplicity I'll extract state from suffix when present and store home_state/away_state where available.\n\nLet me now write all the files. I'll start.\n\nLet me think about the build system. I'll create a `pyproject.toml` (PEP 621) with setuptools so `pip install -e .` works, defining the `brsl` package and console script `brsl-server`. The \"code builds\" requirement → `pip install -e .` should succeed and `python -m brsl.server` or the entry point should work.\n\nLet me also add a `requirements.txt` and note in README.\n\nNow, tests: BDD GWT structured pytest. I'll use the Given/When/Then style with docstrings describing scenarios, and write functional assertions. I'll cover:\n- data loading (all 6 files loadable)\n- match queries (team, date range, competition, season)\n- team statistics (home record, win/loss/draw, goals)\n- head to head\n- player queries (name, nationality, club)\n- competition standings (calculate from matches)\n- statistical analysis (avg goals, biggest wins, home vs away)\n- team name normalization handles variations\n- derbies\n\nLet me create a TODO list and start implementing.\n\nLet me write the files now. I'll be thorough but efficient.\n\nFirst, let me check the exact column dtypes and a couple of gotchas by loading with pandas quickly. Let me check the fifa_data Club values for Brazilian clubs and Nationality == \"Brazil\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0635ed79e001iOL05qpR1mLXOb","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_0635e48ad001jlZ8YEbXGXEqz9"}}
