{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788373832054,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b96253a529549ce6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/brsl/normalization.py","newString":"# Club-name suffix words that may be appended after the distinctive name.\n# They are stripped only when *trailing* (and another token remains), so the\n# distinctive leading token (e.g. the club \"Sport\") is never lost.\n_CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS = {\n    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"scp\",\n    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\",\n}\n\n# Trailing \" - STATE\" or \"-STATE\" or \"-COUNTRY\" patterns.","oldString":"# Suffixes commonly appended to club names that should not be part of the\n# matching key (e.g. \"FC\", \"EC\", \"SC\", \"Sport Club\").\n_NOISE_TOKENS = {\n    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"ss\",\n    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\", \"athletico\",\n    \"athletico\", \"atletico\", \"sport\", \"clubes\", \"scp\",\n}\n\n# Trailing \" - STATE\" or \"-STATE\" or \"-COUNTRY\" patterns."},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n+++ /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n@@ -24,14 +24,14 @@\n     \"MG\", \"PA\", \"PB\", \"PR\", \"PE\", \"PI\", \"RJ\", \"RN\", \"RS\", \"RO\", \"RR\", \"SC\",\n     \"SP\", \"SE\", \"TO\",\n }\n \n-# Suffixes commonly appended to club names that should not be part of the\n-# matching key (e.g. \"FC\", \"EC\", \"SC\", \"Sport Club\").\n-_NOISE_TOKENS = {\n-    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"ss\",\n-    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\", \"athletico\",\n-    \"athletico\", \"atletico\", \"sport\", \"clubes\", \"scp\",\n+# Club-name suffix words that may be appended after the distinctive name.\n+# They are stripped only when *trailing* (and another token remains), so the\n+# distinctive leading token (e.g. the club \"Sport\") is never lost.\n+_CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS = {\n+    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"scp\",\n+    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\",\n }\n \n # Trailing \" - STATE\" or \"-STATE\" or \"-COUNTRY\" patterns.\n _TRAILING_DASH_STATE = re.compile(r\"\\s*-\\s*([A-Z]{2})\\s*$\")\n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/brsl/normalization.py","patch":"Index: /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n+++ /workspace/brsl/normalization.py\n@@ -24,14 +24,14 @@\n     \"MG\", \"PA\", \"PB\", \"PR\", \"PE\", \"PI\", \"RJ\", \"RN\", \"RS\", \"RO\", \"RR\", \"SC\",\n     \"SP\", \"SE\", \"TO\",\n }\n \n-# Suffixes commonly appended to club names that should not be part of the\n-# matching key (e.g. \"FC\", \"EC\", \"SC\", \"Sport Club\").\n-_NOISE_TOKENS = {\n-    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"ss\",\n-    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\", \"athletico\",\n-    \"athletico\", \"atletico\", \"sport\", \"clubes\", \"scp\",\n+# Club-name suffix words that may be appended after the distinctive name.\n+# They are stripped only when *trailing* (and another token remains), so the\n+# distinctive leading token (e.g. the club \"Sport\") is never lost.\n+_CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS = {\n+    \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\", \"cf\", \"scp\",\n+    \"futebol\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\",\n }\n \n # Trailing \" - STATE\" or \"-STATE\" or \"-COUNTRY\" patterns.\n _TRAILING_DASH_STATE = re.compile(r\"\\s*-\\s*([A-Z]{2})\\s*$\")\n","additions":6,"deletions":6},"truncated":false},"title":"brsl/normalization.py","time":{"start":1788373832045,"end":1788373832050}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The normalization mostly works. There are two issues:\n1. \"Sport Club do Recife\" → key \"do recife\" — the noise tokens \"sport\" and \"club\" are stripped but \"do\" remains and \"recife\". For the FIFA data the club is \"Sport Club do Recife\". This means searching for \"Sport\" or \"Sport Club do Recife\" would need to match. The key \"do recife\" is problematic because the matches data has team \"Sport\" (Sport Club do Recife) which normalizes to key... let me check. In Brasileirao the home_team \"Sport\" → key \"sport\"? But I stripped \"sport\" as noise. Hmm. Actually \"Sport\" alone → collapse → \"sport\" but noise tokens removes \"sport\"? Let me check my _NOISE_TOKENS — I included \"sport\". So \"Sport\" → removed entirely → key \"\". That's bad.\n\nThe problem: \"Sport Club do Recife\" is an actual team name where \"Sport\" is the distinctive part (Sport Club do Recife, aka Sport). Stripping \"sport\" and \"club\" as noise breaks this team.\n\nI should be more careful with noise tokens. The intent was to handle things like \"Palmeiras FC\" vs \"Palmeiras\" or \"São Paulo FC\". But stripping \"sport\" entirely breaks \"Sport\" the team.\n\nLet me reconsider. The safest approach: don't aggressively strip tokens. Instead, only strip trailing club-suffix tokens like \"fc\", \"sc\", \"ec\", \"cf\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"sa\" when they appear as the LAST token (common suffixes). Don't strip \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\", \"sport\" globally.\n\nActually, the real need for the key is to match across datasets. Let me look at what variants exist:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" vs \"Palmeiras\" → key \"palmeiras\" both (state stripped) ✓\n- \"Sao Paulo\" vs \"São Paulo\" → accent strip ✓\n- \"FC Barcelona\" vs \"Barcelona\" → strip \"fc\" trailing\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" vs \"Sport\" — in the matches (BR-Football) the team is \"Sport Club do Recife\"; in Brasileirao it's just \"Sport\". Hmm these should match. The key for \"Sport Club do Recife\" should ideally be \"sport\" but my collapse gives \"do recife\". And \"Sport\" gives \"\" (stripped). Both broken.\n\nLet me fix: only strip trailing suffix tokens FC/SC/EC/CF/AC/AA/SA and the words \"club\"/\"esporte\"/\"sporting\" only when they are NOT the leading token. Actually simpler: don't strip \"sport\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"sporting\" at all; only strip the 1-2 letter capital suffixes fc/sc/ec/cf/ac/aa/sa (as a trailing token) and also handle \"club\" / \"esporte\" as a trailing token only.\n\nHmm, but \"Sport Club do Recife\" — to match with \"Sport\", I'd want key \"sport\" for both. That's hard generically. \n\nAlternative approach: instead of aggressive normalization for matching, I'll keep the key as the full stripped name but ALSO generate alternate keys. Actually, the cleanest: the key should be the full normalized name (lowercase, accents stripped, state/country suffix stripped, punctuation/spaces collapsed) WITHOUT removing common words. Then for matching, I'll use a combination of exact key match and \"startswith\"/\"contains\" with a minimum.\n\nBut \"Sao Paulo\" key = \"sao paulo\", \"São Paulo\" key = \"sao paulo\" ✓ (accent strip). \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\" ✓. \"FC Barcelona\" → \"fc barcelona\", \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona\". These won't match exactly. So I need suffix stripping for fc/sc.\n\nLet me define noise stripping more conservatively: strip ONLY trailing tokens that are in {fc, sc, ec, cf, ac, aa, sa, scp, futebol, clube, club, esporte, sporting, sport club} — but only trailing, and only if there remains at least one token after. And never strip the leading token.\n\nSo:\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\": tokens [sport, club, do, recife]. Trailing tokens: \"recife\" not noise. \"do\"? not in noise (I won't include \"do\"). So nothing stripped → key \"sport club do recife\". For \"Sport\" → key \"sport\". These don't match exactly. But contains-match: \"sport\" (5 chars) is in \"sport club do recife\"? My team_matches does `short in long.split()` → \"sport\" in ['sport','club','do','recife'] → True! Good, so \"Sport\" query matches \"Sport Club do Recife\". \n\nBut the reverse: when building the team lookup keyed by team_key, \"Sport Club do Recife\" and \"Sport\" would be different keys → different nodes. For the knowledge graph that's okay-ish but ideally they'd be the same node. For matching queries though, team_matches handles it.\n\nActually for correctness and the spec's \"Handles team name variations correctly\", the contains-match handles it. Let me make the noise stripping conservative (trailing only, preserve leading, only the well-known suffix words) and rely on team_matches substring/token matching for the rest.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I even need noise stripping? The main cross-dataset concern is \"Sao Paulo\" vs \"São Paulo\" (accents — handled) and state suffixes (handled). The FIFA clubs like \"FC Barcelona\" vs match \"Barcelona\" — handled by trailing FC strip + contains. The \"Sport Club do Recife\" vs \"Sport\" — handled by contains.\n\nLet me simplify _collapse: strip accents, lowercase, replace punctuation with space, collapse, then drop ONLY trailing single-letter-ish suffix tokens from {fc, sc, ec, cf, ac, aa, sa} and the trailing words club/esporte/sporting/futebol if they're trailing and not the only token. Keep \"sport\" never stripped (since it's a real team). Keep \"club\"? \"Sport Club do Recife\" — \"club\" is in the middle, trailing is \"recife\", so not stripped. Fine. \"Clube de Regatas do Flamengo\"? Not in our data I think. Let me not over-engineer.\n\nLet me rewrite _collapse to be conservative:\n\n```\ndef _collapse(value):\n    value = _strip_accents(value).strip().lower()\n    value = re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s]\", \" \", value)\n    tokens = value.split()\n    # drop trailing club-suffix tokens, but keep at least one token\n    TRAILING_SUFFIX = {\"fc\",\"sc\",\"ec\",\"cf\",\"ac\",\"aa\",\"sa\",\"scp\",\"futebol\",\"clube\",\"club\",\"esporte\",\"sporting\"}\n    while len(tokens) > 1 and tokens[-1] in TRAILING_SUFFIX:\n        tokens.pop()\n    return \" \".join(tokens)\n```\n\nWait but \"club\" as trailing — \"Sport Club do Recife\" trailing is \"recife\", fine. \"Club Atletico Mineiro\"? trailing \"mineiro\". Fine. But \"Club\" leading like \"Club Bolivar\"? not in data. Okay.\n\nBut what about \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\" — after parenthetical strip in normalize_team, display = \"Boavista Sport Club\". collapse → tokens [boavista, sport, club]. Trailing \"club\" is in suffix → pop → [boavista, sport]. trailing \"sport\" — is \"sport\" in TRAILING_SUFFIX? I excluded \"sport\" from the set (only sport-like words futebol/clube/etc). So key = \"boavista sport\". And the matches data \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...)\" → same → \"boavista sport\". Good, consistent within that dataset.\n\nHmm, but is there a \"Boavista\" elsewhere? Probably not. Fine.\n\nNow \"Sport Club do Recife\": tokens [sport, club, do, recife], trailing \"recife\" not suffix → key = \"sport club do recife\". And \"Sport\" → key = \"sport\". team_matches: short \"sport\" in long.split() → True. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: \"Sport\" could also match \"Esporte\" teams. \"esporte\" → collapse → \"esporte\". \"sport\" in \"esporte\"? `short in long` → \"sport\" in \"esporte\" is False (substring). `short in long.split()` → \"sport\" in ['esporte'] False. Good, no false match.\n\nAnother: \"São Paulo\" key \"sao paulo\". Query \"Sao Paulo FC\"? → collapse strips fc → \"sao paulo\". Good.\n\n\"Corinthians\" → key \"corinthians\". FIFA club \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\"? Let me check actual FIFA club names for Corinthians. Let me not worry; team_matches handles contains.\n\nLet me also reconsider the \"América\" case: \"América - MG\" → display \"América\", key \"america\". But there are multiple América clubs (América-MG, América-RN). They'd both key to \"america\". For matching that's a collision but acceptable for a demo; the spec says handle variations, and disambiguating América-MG vs América-RN would need the state. I keep state separately so the query engine can use state if provided. Good enough.\n\nLet me update _collapse and remove the global _NOISE_TOKENS approach. Let me rewrite that part of normalization.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06363048300146szCNsYJ1Zc3k","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_06361b8bd001AzHfCBLpudcIgV"}}
