on_bucket(label)` does `COMPETITION_BUCKETS.get(str(label))`. When called via Series.map(competition_bucket), it's called per element with a scalar label, so str(scalar) is fine. So `dl.competition_bucket(sub19['competition'])` — calling the function directly with a Series does `str(Series)` once → wrong. I should use `.map`. The test code bug, not library bug.\n\nThe key result: 2019 still shows \"Brasileirao Serie A\" 379 + \"Brasileirao Serie A (2003-2019)\" 74 + \"Serie A\" 170 = 623 total for the brasileirao bucket in 2019. Still overcounted! The dedup by (season, bucket, home, away, hg, ag) didn't collapse the historico 74 and Serie A 170 with brasileirao 379.\n\nWhy? Because the (home_team_key, away_team_key, hg, ag) differ between sources for the same match. Possibly the historico has different scores, or the team keys differ. Let me investigate one specific match: 2019 Palmeiras-Botafogo. brasileirao: 2019-10-12 Palmeiras 1-0 Botafogo. historico: same. br_football Serie A: ? Let me check if br_football has Palmeiras-Botafogo 2019 with same score.\n\nThe dedup should collapse these since bucket+season+home_key+away_key+hg+ag are identical. Unless the keys differ: brasileirao home_team \"Palmeiras\" → key \"palmeiras\"; historico \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\"; br_football \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\". away \"Botafogo\" → \"botafogo\" all. Scores 1-0. So they SHOULD collapse. But the counts show they didn't fully (379+74+170=623).\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that within the brasileirao file itself there are 379 matches in 2019 (one less than 380), and historico 2019 has 380, of which 306 collapsed (380-74=306), and br_football Serie A 2019 has 380 of which 210 collapsed (380-170=210). So 306+210 matched brasileirao, but 74 historico + 170 br_football didn't match brasileirao's set. The non-matching ones have different (home,away,score) — likely because br_football records a DIFFERENT scoreline for the same match (e.g., different final score, or the match is recorded with home/away swapped? No, home/away fixed). Or the team names map to different keys.\n\nHmm, let me directly check: for 2019, count distinct (home_key,away_key,hg,ag) in the brasileirao bucket across all sources. If the same physical match has different scorelines across files, that's a data inconsistency we can't easily reconcile. Let me check a specific case: Palmeiras 2019 home matches across the 3 sources.\n\nActually, let me step back. The overcount from 380 → 623 is 1.64x. For a standings calculation this distorts but the RELATIVE ordering might still be roughly right. But the champion for 2019 Brasileirão should be Flamengo (the spec example says \"Flamengo - 90 pts - Champion\"). If I compute from brasileirao file only (379 matches, 20 teams), I'd get Flamengo as champion. If I compute from the merged 623 (double counted), the points are inflated but Flamengo likely still top. However the point totals would be wrong vs reality.\n\nGiven the spec example explicitly shows \"2019 Brasileirão Final Standings ... 1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L)\", I want standings computed from a single clean source. The cleanest: for standings of \"Brasileirao Serie A\" in season X, use the `brasileirao` source for 2012-2022 and `historico` source for 2003-2011. This avoids cross-source merging entirely. I already have COMPETITION_SOURCE_PREF concept.\n\nSo for competition/season standings AND team season stats, I'll filter to the preferred source for that (competition, season), not the merged dedup. This is the most reliable.\n\nBut for head-to-head and general team stats across all seasons, using dedup is fine (slight overcount acceptable). Actually for head-to-head \"Flamengo 12 wins\" the spec example is just illustrative.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole approach to keep it simple and correct:\n\nApproach: \n- The KnowledgeGraph uses `load_matches_deduplicated()` as its base (good enough, removes the bulk of true duplicates).\n- For standings and team-by-season stats, additionally restrict to a single preferred source per (bucket, season) to guarantee no double count and match real-world point totals.\n\nLet me define a helper `preferred_source_for(bucket, season)`:\n- brasileirao: if season >= 2012 → \"brasileirao\"; else → \"historico\"\n- copa_do_brasil: if season <= 2021 → \"copa_do_brasil\"; else → \"br_football\"\n- libertadores: \"libertadores\" (all seasons; br_football has none)\n- serie_b: \"br_football\"\n- serie_c: \"br_football\"\n\nAnd a function `matches_for(bucket, season)` that returns the deduped frame filtered to that bucket & season & preferred source.\n\nLet me verify 2019 brasileirao from brasileirao source: Flamengo champion with ~90 pts? Let me compute.\n\nLet me implement this in query_engine and verify standings produces Flamengo 2019 champion with reasonable points. Let me check now with a quick script using the brasileirao source for 2019.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063694402001375mOS3Gu3LP1L","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_06368cb4a001kDH3D01YOVSKjh"}}
