{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788374288379,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8ccf14f136642807","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY' 2>&1\nfrom brsl import data_loader as dl\nm = dl.load_matches()\ns19 = m[(m['source']=='brasileirao')&(m['season']==2019)]\nprint(\"Distinct home_team displays:\", s19['home_team'].nunique())\nprint(\"Distinct (home_team, home_state):\", s19[['home_team','home_state']].drop_duplicates().shape[0])\nprint(s19[['home_team','home_team_key','home_state']].drop_duplicates().sort_values('home_team_key').to_string())\nPY"},"output":"Distinct home_team displays: 19\nDistinct (home_team, home_state): 20\n          home_team  home_team_key home_state\n2661       Atletico       atletico         MG\n2667       Atletico       atletico         PR\n2674           Avai           avai         SC\n2666          Bahia          bahia         BA\n2679       Botafogo       botafogo         RJ\n2665          Ceara          ceara         CE\n2662    Chapecoense    chapecoense         SC\n2670    Corinthians    corinthians         SP\n2673       Cruzeiro       cruzeiro         MG\n2671            Csa            csa         AL\n2663       Flamengo       flamengo         RJ\n2669     Fluminense     fluminense         RJ\n2675      Fortaleza      fortaleza         CE\n2677          Goias          goias         GO\n2664         Gremio         gremio         RS\n2672  Internacional  internacional         RS\n2668      Palmeiras      palmeiras         SP\n2678         Santos         santos         SP\n2660      Sao Paulo      sao paulo         SP\n2676  Vasco da Gama  vasco da gama         RJ\n","metadata":{"output":"Distinct home_team displays: 19\nDistinct (home_team, home_state): 20\n          home_team  home_team_key home_state\n2661       Atletico       atletico         MG\n2667       Atletico       atletico         PR\n2674           Avai           avai         SC\n2666          Bahia          bahia         BA\n2679       Botafogo       botafogo         RJ\n2665          Ceara          ceara         CE\n2662    Chapecoense    chapecoense         SC\n2670    Corinthians    corinthians         SP\n2673       Cruzeiro       cruzeiro         MG\n2671            Csa            csa         AL\n2663       Flamengo       flamengo         RJ\n2669     Fluminense     fluminense         RJ\n2675      Fortaleza      fortaleza         CE\n2677          Goias          goias         GO\n2664         Gremio         gremio         RS\n2672  Internacional  internacional         RS\n2668      Palmeiras      palmeiras         SP\n2678         Santos         santos         SP\n2660      Sao Paulo      sao paulo         SP\n2676  Vasco da Gama  vasco da gama         RJ\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY' 2>&1\nfrom brsl import data_loader as dl\nm = dl.load_matches()\ns19 = m[(m['source']=='brasileirao')&(m['season']==2019)]\nprint(\"Distinct home_team displays:\", s19['home_team'].nunique())\nprint(\"Distinct (home_team, home_state):\", s19[['home_team','home_state']].drop_duplicates().shape[0])\nprint(s19[['home_team','home_team_key','home_state']].drop_duplicates().sort_values('home_team_key').to_string())\nPY","time":{"start":1788374288269,"end":1788374288370}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting — the spec example said \"1. Flamengo - 90 pts (28W, 6D, 4L) - Champion\" and we get \"Flamengo - 90 pts (28W 6D 4L)\" — exactly matching! But \"Atletico - 112 pts (31W 19D 26L)\" appears above with 76 games?? That's because \"Atletico\" key collides multiple Atlético clubs (Atlético-MG, Atlético-GO, Atlético-PR) into one team_key \"atletico\". So all Atlético* teams' matches are merged → 76 games, 112 pts. That's the América/Atlético collision problem.\n\nSo the team_key collision for \"atletico\" (Atlético Mineiro, Atlético Paranaense, Atlético Goianiense) is a real problem. The Brasileirao 2019 has 20 teams but we got 19 distinct home_team_keys, meaning two Atlético teams collapsed to \"atletico\". Indeed 2019 Brasileirao had Atlético-MG and Athletico-PR (written \"Athletico\" in 2019? Actually in 2019 it was still \"Athletico Paranaense\" sometimes \"Atlético Paranaense\"). Let me check the home_team values for key \"atletico\" in 2019.\n\nThis is the disambiguation challenge. The spec notes \"Handles team name variations correctly\". For the Atletico/Atlético collision, I need to use the state suffix to disambiguate. \"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético-PR\" → states MG vs PR.\n\nSo my team_key should incorporate the state when there's a collision, OR I should keep teams distinct by (key, state) when state is available.\n\nBut the historico file has separate Mandante_UF columns and the team name without suffix. The brasileirao file has team name WITH state suffix and separate state column. So state IS available for brasileirao and historico. For br_football there's no state. For fifa, no state.\n\nFor the standings use case (brasileirao source 2019), state is available in home_state/away_state. So I should key teams by (key, state) when state present, OR append state to the key for disambiguation.\n\nBetter approach: when building the team_key for matching, append the state to the key when the base key is ambiguous. But the base key \"atletico\" with state MG → \"atletico mg\". Then \"Atlético-MG\" → key \"atletico mg\" and \"Atlético-PR\" → \"atletico pr\". They become distinct. And a query \"Atlético Mineiro\" → key \"atletico mineiro\" — won't match \"atletico mg\" exactly but team_matches contains would match? \"atletico mineiro\" vs \"atletico mg\": short \"atletico\" in long.split() True → matches both Atlético-MG and Atlético-PR. Ambiguous. For a query like \"Atlético-MG\" → key \"atletico mg\" matches \"atletico mg\" exactly. Good.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe cleanest disambiguation: use (base_key, state) as the composite identity for teams where state is known. The display stays the accented base. For matching, a query without state that is ambiguous returns multiple teams (the find_teams already returns a list). For standings I iterate the deduped frame using (home_team_key, home_state) composite.\n\nBut wait — my current home_team_key strips the state. Let me change the keying to incorporate state when available, producing keys like \"palmeiras\" (no state needed, unambiguous) and \"atletico mg\"/\"atletico pr\" (disambiguated). But that would break matches where one file has state and another doesn't (e.g., brasileirao \"Atlético-MG\" → \"atletico mg\", br_football \"Atletico Mineiro\" → \"atletico mineiro\" — these won't match \"atletico mg\"). \n\nTradeoff. For standings I use brasileirao source which has state, so composite key (base, state) works and disambiguates Atlético-MG vs Atlético-PR. \n\nLet me check 2019 Brasileirao: how many distinct (home_team_key, home_state) and what are the \"atletico\" entries.\n\nActually, the simplest fix that makes the spec example pass: in the standings, use the ORIGINAL home_team display (which in brasileirao includes state like \"Atlético-MG\") as the team identity, NOT the normalized key. I.e., group by the source-specific display name. That way \"Atlético-MG\" and \"Athletico-PR\" (or \"Atlético-PR\") are distinct because their display strings differ.\n\nBut across files the display differs (\"Atlético-MG\" vs \"Atlético Mineiro\"). For a single-source standings that's fine.\n\nFor the KnowledgeGraph teams dict keyed by team_key, the collision means Atletico-MG and Atletico-PR share a node \"atletico\" — wrong for stats. \n\nOK here's my refined plan:\n- Introduce a `team_identity` that is `(base_key, state_or_country)` when state/country is present, else just `base_key`. This disambiguates Atletico-MG/PR while keeping Palmeiras as \"palmeiras\".\n- Use `team_identity` for grouping in standings and team stats.\n- For matching queries, `find_teams(query)` uses base_key + team_matches; if the base_key is ambiguous (multiple states), return all and let the caller (or a disambiguation hint) pick. For standings/season-stats I use team_identity from the frame.\n\nBut the home_team_key currently is just base_key. Let me add a `home_team_id` column = base_key + (\" \" + state if state) ... no, let me make a composite. Actually let me just change home_team_key to include state when present.\n\nHmm, but then \"Palmeiras-SP\" → key \"palmeiras sp\" and a query \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" won't equal \"palmeiras sp\"; team_matches contains would match (short \"palmeiras\" in long \"palmeiras sp\".split() → True). OK that works for matching. But it means Palmeiras-SP and Palmeiras (no state) are different identities — fine since Palmeiras is unambiguous.\n\nBut the issue: br_football \"Palmeiras\" has no state → identity \"palmeiras\". brasileirao \"Palmeiras-SP\" → identity \"palmeiras sp\". These differ! For dedup by (bucket, season, home_id, away_id, hg, ag) they wouldn't collapse → overcount persists. \n\nArgh. The state availability is inconsistent across files.\n\nOK, let me think differently about dedup. The dedup is causing headaches. Let me reconsider whether I even need cross-source dedup for the SUCCESS CRITERIA.\n\nThe success criteria:\n- All 6 CSV files loadable & queryable ✓ (raw load_matches)\n- 20+ sample questions answerable\n- Cross-file queries work (player + match data)\n- Standings example: \"2019 Brasileirão Final Standings ... Flamengo 90 pts champion\"\n\nFor standings, I'll compute from a SINGLE preferred source per (bucket, season) — this sidesteps dedup entirely and matches reality. For team stats / head-to-head / search_matches, I'll use the raw per-source or a per-source query, and accept that \"in dataset\" totals reflect the primary source.\n\nActually, re-reading the spec's head-to-head example: \"Flamengo vs Fluminense (Fla-Flu derby): ... Head-to-head in dataset: Flamengo 12 wins, Fluminense 8 wins, 7 draws\" — this looks like it's from ONE source (the brasileirao file). So using a single preferred source for head-to-head is also fine and matches the example's spirit.\n\nSo my new, simpler, more-correct plan:\n- Keep `load_matches()` (raw, 23954) and `load_matches_deduplicated()` (best-effort, ~19510) available.\n- The KnowledgeGraph uses `load_matches_deduplicated()` as the base for graph nodes.\n- For standings, season team stats, head-to-head, biggest wins in a season/competition, average goals per competition-season: use a `select_competition_season(bucket, season)` helper that picks the SINGLE preferred source, guaranteeing clean non-double-counted results that match real point totals.\n- For general \"search matches\" and \"what competitions has Palmeiras played in\", use the deduped frame (or raw) — duplicates acceptable there since the user is browsing.\n\nAnd the Atlético disambiguation for standings: since I use the brasleirao source which has state, I'll group by display name (which includes state \"Atlético-MG\") — distinct. Let me verify the brasleirao 2019 has distinct display names for the two Atlético teams.\n\nLet me check the home_team display values for key 'atletico' in brasleirao 2019.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06369fdff001zyP4px3CDhyWkZ","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_063695baa001Ffb4x5P4IvrKzN"}}
