m_frame uses team_matches(team, key). team=\"Corinthians\" → key \"corinthians\". team_matches('Corinthians', 'corinthians') → collapse both \"corinthians\" → equal → True. So home filter keeps 19. But got 15.\n\nHmm. Then stats_for iterates rows and skips if pd.isna(home_goal) or pd.isna(away_goal). Maybe 4 rows have NaN goals? Let me check Corinthians home 2022 for NaN goals. Actually wait — the discrepancy could be that `_frame_for_bucket_season` for brasileirao+2022 returned the preferred source 'brasileirao' (380 matches) — but then stats_for counts only rows where goals not NaN. Are there 4 Corinthians home 2022 matches with NaN goals? Let me check.\n\nActually wait — re-examine: my team_stats with competition='brasileirao', season=2022: `_frame_for_bucket_season(df, bucket='brasileirao', season=2022)`. Inside: labels = BUCKET_LABELS['brasileirao'] = ['Brasileirao Serie A', 'Brasileirao Serie A (2003-2019)', 'Serie A']. sub = df where competition in labels. season==2022 → sub = those rows in 2022. Then preferred_source('brasileirao', 2022) = 'brasileirao' (since 2022 > 2011). candidate = sub[source=='brasileirao']. If not empty use it. So we get brasileirao 2022 = 380. Good.\n\nThen team_rows = _team_frame(sub_380, 'Corinthians', state=None). state is None because normalize_team('Corinthians') has no state. So _team_frame returns rows where home_team_key matches OR away_team_key matches → 38 rows.\n\nThen venue='home': team_rows = team_rows[home_team_key matches] → 19 rows.\n\nThen stats_for iterates 19 rows, skips NaN goals. Result matches=15 means 4 rows skipped due to NaN goals!\n\nLet me check Corinthians home 2022 NaN goals in brasileirao source.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0636ddd77001uWjPJHJ0QR71bj","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_0636dafc8001tLiAQEz7P0RwEc"}}
