{\"limit\":3})\n    print(\"top_brazilian_players:\", json.dumps(res2, default=str)[:400])\nasyncio.run(main())\nPY","time":{"start":1788374568013,"end":1788374568019}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The 4 NaN-goal Corinthians home matches (rounds 31, 34, 36, 38) are late-season 2022 matches with missing scores. These are matches that hadn't been recorded / future or missing in the dataset. The spec example says \"19 matches, 11W 5D 3L, GF 28 GA 15\". With 4 missing, we have 15 matches: let me count W/D/L from the 15 with goals: \n- 3-0 W, 1-0 W, 1-1 D, 1-1 D, 2-0 W, 1-0 W, 0-0 D, 1-0 W, 3-1 W, 1-0 W, 0-1 L, 1-0 W, 2-2 D, 2-1 W, 2-0 W\nThat's W: 3-0,1-0,2-0,1-0,1-0,3-1,1-0,1-0,2-1,2-0 = 10 wins. D: 1-1,1-1,0-0,2-2 = 4 draws. L: 0-1 = 1 loss. Total 15. Matches my engine output (10W 4D 1L). \n\nThe spec example's 11W 5D 3L over 19 matches is the FULL season including those 4 — but those 4 have no score data so they can't be classified. The spec example is illustrative; our data has 4 missing. This is a data-quality note, not a bug. My engine correctly reports the 15 with known scores.\n\nThis is fine for the benchmark — the data simply lacks those 4 results. The test should assert based on actual data (15 matches with known scores, 10W). I'll write tests asserting on actual computed values, not the spec's illustrative numbers, but I'll assert the champion/standings which DO match exactly.\n\nActually, to make the spec's headline example (standings 2019 Flamengo 90) pass, that's solid because 2019 has all 380 results.\n\nSo my engine is correct. The 15 vs 19 is a data gap. Good.\n\nNow let me also double check the `derbies 2023 count = 22`. The spec example \"Show me all derbies in 2023\". 22 is fine.\n\nNow, there's a concern in `search_matches` with both team and opponent: I have convoluted logic. Let me simplify and verify it returns the right thing. Actually the head_to_head already covers the vs case. search_matches with team+opponent should return matches between them. My current logic is messy but the test showed count=77 for Flamengo vs Fluminense (matches the head_to_head 77). Good, it works.\n\nWait, but search_matches count 77 == head_to_head 77. But search_matches uses self.df (deduped graph frame) and head_to_head also uses self.df. Both 77. Good consistent.\n\nBut note: search_matches Flamengo vs Fluminense without competition uses the full deduped frame → includes Flamengo-Fluminense matches from brasileirao, copa, libertadores, br_football. 77 total. Good.\n\nNow let me also verify the MCP server builds (lists tools) and that I can call a tool via the MCPServer API. Let me test build_server and list_tools.\n\nLet me also make sure `python -m brsl.server` or the entry point runs (it would block on stdio). I'll just test build_server and call_tool for one tool.\n\nLet me test the server.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0636e2d24001PCpsMiqN7yPnau","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_0636dfa5f0010uaRmbBAEJgZ46"}}
