saly']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY' 2>&1\nfrom brsl import data_loader as dl\np = dl.load_players()\nprint(\"Clubs containing 'Flam':\")\nprint(p[p['Club'].astype(str).str.contains('Flam', case=False, na=False)]['Club'].value_counts().to_string())\nprint(\"Clubs containing 'Botafogo':\")\nprint(p[p['Club'].astype(str).str.contains('Botafogo', case=False, na=False)]['Club'].value_counts().to_string())\nprint(\"Distinct Brazilian-ish clubs sample:\")\nprint(sorted(p['Club'].dropna().unique().tolist())[:30])\nPY","time":{"start":1788374749475,"end":1788374749582}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"4 failures to fix:\n\n1. `test_champion_2019`: `champ[\"record\"]` is a tuple `(28,6,4)` but test expects list `[28,6,4]`. My champion returns `record: (top[\"wins\"], top[\"draws\"], top[\"losses\"])` which is a tuple. JSON serialization would make it a list, but in the test the engine returns a Python dict (not serialized), so it's a tuple. Fix: make champion return a list, OR fix the test to compare tuple. Let me make champion return list for consistency. Change to `[top[\"wins\"], top[\"draws\"], top[\"losses\"]]`.\n\n2. `test_leading_fc_prefix`: `normalize_team(\"FC Barcelona\")` → display \"FC Barcelona\", not \"Barcelona\". My regex `^([A-Z]{2})\\s+` matches \"FC \" — but \"FC\" is in `_CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS`? _CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS = {\"fc\",\"sc\",\"ec\",...} lowercase. But the regex checks `m.group(1) in _CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS` where group(1) is \"FC\" (uppercase). The set contains lowercase \"fc\". \"FC\" not in {\"fc\",...}. So no strip. Fix: compare case-insensitively: `m.group(1).lower() in _CLUB_SUFFIX_TOKENS`.\n\n3. `test_search_players_by_club`: `search_players(club=\"Flamengo\")` returns count 0. But FIFA data has club \"Flamengo\" (we saw Flamengo players earlier). Why 0? The `search_players` mask: `players[\"Club\"].astype(str).str.contains(\"Flamengo\", ...)`. The FIFA Club for Flamengo is \"Flamengo\" exactly. So should match. count = int(players[mask].shape[0])... wait I compute `count = int(players[mask].shape[0])` but `mask` was modified after sub. Let me look: in search_players I do `mask = pd.Series(True,...)`, then apply filters including `mask &= players[\"Club\"]...contains(club)`. Then `sub = players[mask]`, sort, head. Then `count = int(players[mask].shape[0])`. That should be the masked count. If club filter yields 0, then Flamengo isn't found. But earlier `players_at_brazilian_clubs` showed clubs including... let me check: did \"Flamengo\" appear in FIFA Club column? Earlier sample of top Brazilian clubs didn't list Flamengo in top 15 (it listed Grêmio, Atlético Mineiro, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Santos, Internacional, América FC, Botafogo, Bahia, Parana, Atlético Paranaense, Vitória, Sport Club do Recife, Chapecoense, Ceará). Flamengo wasn't there! So maybe FIFA data doesn't have \"Flamengo\" as a club (perhaps Flamengo players are listed under a different club or Flamengo isn't in this FIFA snapshot's leagues). Let me check.\n\nActually the spec example: \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" and \"Which players play for Flamengo? Filter FIFA data by Club containing 'Flamengo'\". So the spec assumes Flamengo is in FIFA data. Let me verify whether \"Flamengo\" exists in the Club column.\n\nLet me check the Club column values containing \"Flam\" or \"Flamengo\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06370f72b001f56keQpsl99FhT","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","messageID":"msg_06370c360001zmbBq2AJuGUZ31"}}
