{"type":"text","timestamp":1788375086819,"sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","part":{"id":"prt_0637627f6001KZLU5u1AnxRxgj","messageID":"msg_06376209d001tfqWay0P5OP3Sd","sessionID":"ses_f9ca2d3d8ffe7GbwdSu72bCYg1","type":"text","text":"Done. Implemented the Brazilian Soccer MCP server per `TASK.md`:\n\n**Build & tests:** `pip install -e .` succeeds; `pytest` → **59 passed**, all queries well within the spec's <2s/<5s limits.\n\n**Code (`brsl/` package, all written to `/workspace`):**\n- `normalization.py` — collapses the datasets' many team-name variants (state suffixes, foreign country codes, accents, `FC` prefixes) into a canonical key, preserving state for disambiguation (`Atlético-MG` vs `-PR`).\n- `data_loader.py` — loads all 6 CSVs into one unified match frame + FIFA players; handles ISO/Brazilian dates & UTF-8/BOM; provides de-duplication so the same match appearing across files isn't double-counted.\n- `knowledge_graph.py` — in-memory `Team`/`Player`/`Match`/`Competition` graph (no external DB needed).\n- `query_engine.py` — all 5 capability areas (match/team/player/competition/statistics) + head-to-head + derbies; JSON-serialisable results.\n- `server.py` — MCP server (`mcp` v2 SDK) exposing **18 tools**, runnable via `brsl-server` / `python -m brsl.server` over stdio.\n\n**Verification highlights (match the spec's example answers exactly):** 2019 Brasileirão champion = Flamengo, 90 pts (28W/6D/4L); relegated = Cruzeiro/CSA/Chapecoense/Avai; top Brazilian player = Neymar Jr (92); 20-team standings with the two Atléticos separated.\n\nNote: `prompts.txt` was ignored per its own instruction; the spec didn't mandate Neo4j (only the sample prompt did), so the knowledge graph is in-process to keep the benchmark self-contained. README.md updated to document the implementation. No git commit was made since you didn't ask.","time":{"start":1788375082998,"end":1788375086815}}}
